  {"type":"culture","title":"Πώς τα 1821, 1922 και 1974 πλάθουν τη σημερινή συζήτηση","text":"**1821.** 25 March is a double holiday (national and religious: the Annunciation), with school and military parades. Every few years a textbook or TV series sparks a row over the 'myths' of the Revolution — the secret school (*κρυφό σχολειό*), the role of the Church, the massacres at Tripolitsa — and the old tension the text describes, **Έλληνας** versus **Ρωμιός**, ancient glory versus Orthodox continuity, is still live (*Ρωμιοσύνη* is a Ritsos poem and a whole worldview). The 2021 bicentennial reopened all of it.\n\n**1922.** Refugee place names are everywhere: *Νέα Σμύρνη, Νέα Ιωνία, Νέα Φιλαδέλφεια, Καισαριανή*. Families still say *είμαστε Μικρασιάτες / Πόντιοι*. 14 September is the official day of remembrance, 19 May for the Pontic Greeks. In 2006–07 a primary textbook that described the scene on Smyrna's quay as a *συνωστισμός* 'crowding' was withdrawn after a national outcry: the word itself became shorthand for revisionism.\n\n**1974.** Politicians and commentators still define themselves against *η Μεταπολίτευση*: for some it is the period that finally gave Greece stable democracy and Europe; for others it is the era of *πελατειακό κράτος* 'clientelist state' and party patronage that led to the 2010 bankruptcy. The **17 November** march to the US embassy (the 1973 Polytechnic uprising) and the long fight over **university asylum** (*πανεπιστημιακό άσυλο*) are rituals of that same memory. If you hear *τα παιδιά της Μεταπολίτευσης*, it means the generation raised after 1974."}
